    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.pairs\n\n### Description\n\nI think it would be neat if mini.pairs supported the japanese versions of ([{}]), I tried the following settings:\n```lua\nrequire('mini.pairs').setup({\n  mappings = {\n    [\"（\"] = { action = \"open\", pair = \"（）\", neigh_pattern = \"[^\\\\].\" },\n    [\"「\"] = { action = \"open\", pair = \"「」\", neigh_pattern = \"[^\\\\].\" },\n    [\"｛\"] = { action = \"open\", pair = \"｛｝\", neigh_pattern = \"[^\\\\].\" },\n\n    [\"）\"] = { action = \"close\", pair = \"（）\", neigh_pattern = \"[^\\\\].\" },\n    [\"」\"] = { action = \"close\", pair = \"「」\", neigh_pattern = \"[^\\\\].\" },\n    [\"｝\"] = { action = \"close\", pair = \"｛｝\", neigh_pattern = \"[^\\\\].\" },\n\n})\n```\nBut it doesn't seem to recognize them and surrounds both sides with whichever bracket I use first:\n```lua\n-- I select abc\nabc\n-- I do gsa to add a surrounding pair and type 「\n「abc「\n-- I don't get 「abc」\n```\nMaybe it has something to do with these brackets being unicode and not being one byte.",
